.TH stty 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
stty \- set or show terminal settings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stty
.br
.B stty \-a
.br
.B stty \-g
.br
.B stty
.I "setting ..."
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no arguments,
.B stty
shows the settings of the terminal on standard input that differ from
the defaults.  With settings, it changes them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Show every setting, not just the ones that differ.
.TP
.B \-g
Print the settings as four numbers, in a form that can be given back to
.B stty
later to restore them.
.SH SETTINGS
Each may be given as
.I name
to turn it on or
.BI \- name
to turn it off.
.SS Speed
.B ispeed
.IR n ,
.B ospeed
.IR n ,
or a bare number to set both.
.SS Control
.BR clocal ,
.BR cread ,
.BR cstopb ,
.BR hupcl ,
.BR parenb ,
.BR parodd .
.SS Input
.BR brkint ,
.BR icrnl ,
.BR ignbrk ,
.BR igncr ,
.BR ignpar ,
.BR inlcr ,
.BR inpck ,
.BR istrip ,
.BR ixany ,
.BR ixoff ,
.BR ixon ,
.BR parmrk .
.SS Output
.BR opost ,
.BR onlcr ,
.BR onoeot ,
.BR xtabs .
.SS Local
.BR echo ,
.BR echoe ,
.BR echok ,
.BR echonl ,
.BR icanon ,
.BR iexten ,
.BR isig ,
.BR lflusho ,
.BR noflsh ,
.BR tostop .
.SS Control characters
.BR eof ,
.BR eol ,
.BR erase ,
.BR intr ,
.BR kill ,
.BR quit ,
.BR susp ,
.BR start ,
.BR stop ,
.BR rprnt ,
.BR lnext ,
.BR flush ,
each followed by the character, which may be written as
.BI ^ x
for a control character or
.B undef
to disable it.  Also
.B min
and
.BR time ,
which are numbers.
.SS Combinations
.TP
.B cooked
The normal line-at-a-time mode, with echo and signals.
.TP
.B raw
Character at a time, no echo, no signal characters.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# stty -a
# stty erase ^H
# stty raw
# stty cooked
.fi
.SH NOTES
.B stty
acts on standard input, so it is the terminal you type the command on
unless you redirect.  To set another one:
.B stty raw < /dev/tty2
.PP
There is no
.B sane
setting.  If you leave a terminal in raw mode and lose the echo,
.B stty cooked
typed blind will bring it back.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tty (1),
.BR who (1)
